cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
authors:
- family-names: "Hörtnagl"
  given-names: "Lukas"
  orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5569-0761"
  affiliation: "Grassland Sciences group, ETH Zürich"
title: "dyco"
abstract: "Time-lag detection and compensation for eddy covariance raw data, using pre-whitening
  with block-bootstrap so that every detected lag carries an uncertainty interval."
version: 3.0.0
date-released: 2026-08-06
# SPDX. Not the bare "GPL-3.0", which SPDX deprecated. LICENSE grants version 3 "or (at your
# option) any later version", so this is the identifier that matches it.
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
url: "https://github.com/holukas/dyco"
repository-code: "https://github.com/holukas/dyco"
keywords:
  - eddy covariance
  - time lag
  - flux
  - micrometeorology
  - greenhouse gases

# DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and both were here when Zenodo refused this release twice with an empty
# error body:
#
#   doi: / identifiers:  the concept DOI, 10.5281/zenodo.4964067. Zenodo mints a fresh version DOI
#       at every release and already knows the concept it belongs to. Declaring that same DOI in
#       the metadata of the record being created is self-referential, and Zenodo does not need it.
#       Cite the concept DOI from the README and the docs, not from here.
#
#   references:  the 2021 JOSS article. It describes v1.1.2 and a method v3 removed, so it is not
#       the citation for this software in any case; docs/migrating-from-v2.md carries it in full.
#
# The last file that Zenodo accepted (v2.0.3, May 2025) had neither, and nothing but authors,
# title and url besides. Everything above that line is descriptive metadata Zenodo maps directly.
# If a future release fails the same way, strip back to that shape before looking anywhere else.
